# HW-019 (final) - ES8311 rate sweep + route transitions, with the SAFETY fixes.
# 2026-07-12, StickS3 on COM35 (ESP32-S3-PICO-1-N8R8, MAC 98:88:e0:0e:dc:64).
#
# This is the run that validates the fixes an adversarial review found AFTER the
# first PASS, and it is the one that counts. Three things changed in the driver:
#
#   1. stop_output() is no longer gated on the route. It was a FAIL-OPEN: a
#      configure() that dies on its first register write changes nothing on the
#      chip -- an old route's DAC still powered, still unmuted, still driving the
#      amplifier -- but the route cache had already been cleared, so the caller
#      reaching for the off switch found it doing nothing at all. Muting is a
#      safety operation and is idempotent; it is never gated now. UNMUTING still
#      is. And the ztest that asserted "writes nothing after a failed configure"
#      was not testing correct behaviour, it was pinning the fail-open.
#
#   2. configure() quiesces BEFORE it moves the clock tree: both serial ports
#      muted, the DAC muted and powered down, and only then the clock manager and
#      the dividers. Gating the clock of a DAC that is still powered and unmuted
#      does not silence it, it freezes the modulator on the last sample -- a DC
#      step into the amplifier.
#
#   3. The ADC is NOT power-cycled in that quiesce, only muted at its serial port.
#      It drives no speaker and cannot pop, and power-cycling it on every
#      configure cost a DC offset: measured, the capture noise floor rose from
#      about 70 counts to about 500. Muting the port is the whole of what it needs.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: the register-0x00 software reset.
#
# The binding claimed the driver "relies on the software reset in register 0x00".
# It did not -- CSM_ON is a different bit in the same register and resets nothing --
# so the claim was false and the review was right to call it a blocker. The fix
# looked obvious: implement the reset the reference drivers use. On this hardware
# it DEAFENS THE ADC:
#
#     with the reset      8000  adc=DEAD floor=0    tone_rms=0
#                        16000  adc=DEAD floor=1    tone_rms=10
#                        22050  adc=alive floor=6   tone_rms=73
#                        48000  adc=alive floor=573 tone_rms=15372
#
#     without it (below)  8000  adc=alive floor=75  tone_rms=1935
#                        48000  adc=alive floor=63  tone_rms=14724
#
# The captured level tracks the tone's frequency in Hz, not its rate: the tone table
# is fixed, so at 8 kHz it plays at 220 Hz and at 48 kHz at 1320 Hz. That is a
# high-pass with a corner near 1 kHz appearing from nowhere. Initialising 0x0B and
# 0x0C after the reset, as Linux and ESP-ADF both do, does not change it. The
# registers the driver writes all read back exactly as intended, at every rate.
#
# The root cause is not established, so the reset is NOT shipped and the BINDING is
# corrected to say what the driver actually does and why. Shipping a reset that
# silently halves the capture bandwidth, to make a sentence in a binding come true,
# would be the exact failure this project exists to avoid.
#
# RESULT below: 9/9 rates PASS, 3/3 routes PASS, restore measured at 16000 Hz, and
# the noise floor is back where it was (52-129, against 58-94 before any of this).
#
